near differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2ax{y}_{1}-2bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}]+b[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the r